 "schema": "terluna.atmosphere.rain-fall-law/1",
 "evidence": "A runnable calculation: the single-drop solver's terminal speeds, which reproduce Earth's measured fall speeds to 0.20 m/s, in the Moon's air at 2 m in the resort, integrated over Morrison's exponential spectra. The quadrature reproduces the closed forms of a power law to within 3e-6. The self-collection rate carries Seifert and Beheng's Earth constant by the ratio of geometric collision kernels with collision efficiency 1; no lunar collision has been measured, and no CM1 run has tested the law.",
 "reading_rule": "table holds, on ln lambda from ln_lambda_first in steps of ln_lambda_step (lambda in 1/m), the natural logs of the mass-weighted and number-weighted mean fall speeds (m/s) and of the ventilation integral (the integral of D^(3/2) V^(1/2) exp(-lambda D) dD, m3 s^-1/2) of rain's exponential spectrum in Morrison's reference air; the scheme multiplies the speeds by its density factor and the integral by its square root, and interpolates linearly in ln lambda, holding the ends. comparison gives, by mean diameter 1/lambda, the present law's moments and the best power law's over the solver's. self_collection gives the kernel ratio, Earth's over the Moon's, and the lunar rate. namelist holds the settings CM1's rain-law patch (climate/crm/cm1_run.py) takes.",
 "assumptions": [
  "The speeds are the solver's at 2 m in the resort's air at 301 K and 1.2 atm. Morrison's density factor (RHOSU / rho)^0.54 carries them to the air above, whose density through the lowest 10 km stays within about a tenth of the ground's.",
  "Below 1 um the speeds follow Stokes's D^2, which the solver meets there to 0.1%; beyond the stability screen, where Morrison's untruncated spectrum still places mass, they hold at the screen's speed, the largest drop's.",
  "Self-collection takes the geometric kernel with collision efficiency 1 between 80 um and Earth's stability screen, in each world's air at 2 m; its breakup offset is the rain limits'."
 ],
